 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1039 "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186221175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1045 "Equilibrium Points and Stability for Systems of ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186222315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "081b50ee-0118-5b55-ada7-29231441c411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222315 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222315 #edb/ref 1061 588701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222315 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c2ee65c6-624f-52a8-8b0a-ed23e84593bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222315 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222316 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222316 #edb/ref 1061 588702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222316 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2be51b6-d1cc-5e3f-8242-1dc23fa98c20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222316 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222317 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222317 #edb/ref 1061 655321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222317 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2756df62-6611-50b8-b90b-649614f0beca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222317 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222318 #edb/ref 1061 588706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64234359-7fbc-5b3b-b380-92c84702a88e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222490 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that for $x'(t) = f(x),$ a point $x^\\ast$ is an *equilibrium* if $f(x^\\ast) = 0.$ Solutions starting at $x^\\ast$ remain constant for all $t.$\n\nNow, consider a system in the plane with variables $x(t)$ and $y(t){:}$\n![](images/92/92ee0672fab8119d72b73787d926a36b921e62ab18b5b6c782af901c7a1e491c.svg)\n\nA point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = (x^\\ast, y^\\ast)$ is an **equilibrium point** if $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast) = \\mathbf{0}.$ Both components must be zero:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}^{∗},{y}^{∗})=0 \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}^{∗},{y}^{∗})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ then $\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ for all $t.$ The state does not change.\n\n**Note:** In the phase plane, $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ represents velocity. At an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ the velocity is $\\mathbf{0},$ so trajectories starting there stay there forever.\n\nFor example, let's find the equilibrium points of the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=x-y \\\\ {y}^{′}=x+y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** Set the right-hand sides equal to zero.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=0 \\\\ x+y-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Solve the system. From the first equation, $y = x.$ Substitute into the second:\n\n$$\nx + (x) - 2 = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 2x = 2 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x = 1.\n$$\n\nThus, $y = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the system has a single equilibrium point ![](images/59/5939547026b84f5a36fd2f26d7390db4c5069cdf3302217b5309699e5afe8882.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222490 #edb/ref 1065 14487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222490 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7af2084d-7c8b-577e-b656-d1040dddaabd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222490 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222491 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we have learned how to find equilibrium points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y).\n$$\n\nHowever, just knowing where equilibrium points are is not enough: we\nalso want to understand *how nearby solutions behave* when we start\nclose to an equilibrium point.\n\nRoughly speaking, there are three qualitatively different behaviors.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **stable** if for\nany neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ there exists a neighborhood\n$O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at\nsome time $t = t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently\nclose to the equilibrium remain close to it for all later times,\nalthough they need not converge to it.\n\n![](images/ca/ca05b3e5d076c74f4d3cf346e0598e99d363a155d41ef10b98d1914602638b76.png)\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **asymptotically\nstable** if it is stable *and* there exists a neighborhood $O$ of\n$\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$\napproaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently\nclose to the equilibrium remain close to it *and* converge to it as\n$t \\to \\infty.$\n\nNote that asymptotic stability is a stronger condition than stability:\nit adds the requirement that nearby solutions actually reach\n$\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ in the limit.\n\n![](images/ce/cedd8e777a57901042cb46889650006a9b6e1980dc733d736790e3cc3e3ccfaa.png)\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **unstable** if it\nis not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means there is some neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ from which trajectories can escape, even when started\narbitrarily close to $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ Note that this does not\nrequire trajectories to stay outside $O$ forever; it only requires them to\nleave $O$ at some moment, even if they return later.\n\n![](images/f7/f7668e3f5ba2b8dc7b4ca3a3e503c55a54a00c2f99648895e052447274bb6d15.png)\n\n**Watch out!** These descriptions are *local*: they describe what\nhappens when the initial condition is sufficiently close to the\nequilibrium. They do not require that all trajectories in the entire\nplane behave the same way.\n\nNext, let's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222491 #edb/ref 1065 15932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222491 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "398c0856-505e-5ecb-b56f-1a57b0837d91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222491 #edb/ref 1067 "Stability, Asymptotic Stability, and Instability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305330 #edb/ref 1080 "Stable but not asymptotically stable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305330 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305330 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14e0b670-47dc-5a81-b6a3-73480799b684" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305337 #edb/ref 1080 "Unstable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305337 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305337 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "155e1b96-9f12-5a78-8177-2ef3dd7dffe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305412 #edb/ref 1080 "Asymptotically stable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305412 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305412 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1c49b675-9a33-5a1b-bfe1-fb87827512d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306774 #edb/ref 1080 "Asymptotically stable only for nonlinear systems." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306774 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306774 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ac0cf04c-3ed2-5c47-b074-fbdb27ec7e6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306957 #edb/ref 1080 "Asymptotically unstable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306957 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306957 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c141bb23-13e7-5a1f-9653-dfc26c953e10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308257 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308257 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308257 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308257 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308257 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308257 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308257 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308257 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308257 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea866ddf-d714-574d-89e8-b458a481e7d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308445 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308445 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308445 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308445 #edb/ref 1071 "A small perturbation away from an equilibrium causes trajectories to slowly spiral towards the equilibrium. How should the equilibrium be classified?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dbea5a9-54c6-5f65-9a92-c413f9e3360e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308445 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Asymptotically stable.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308762 #edb/ref 1080 "but move away from" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308762 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308762 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0216fb78-36e7-5fd7-9246-3e737defdfce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308972 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308972 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308972 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1d0af402-a3d6-5494-a083-1e02c24805bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309135 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186309135 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309135 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "32ceebdb-d7b6-51b7-86a2-d214dea3680b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309215 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186309215 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309215 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d82b4e8-7462-56ad-9