68 #edb/ref 17592186311176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311598 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311598 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311598 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point.\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently \n{{field-1}}\nthe equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium\n{{field-2}} \nthe \n{{field-3}} \nas \n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311598 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d70bc07a-1c80-562a-90f6-efe6dcc9cf2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311598 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently $\\text{close to}$ the equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium $\\text{and converge to}$ the $\\text{equilibrium}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311602 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311602 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335470" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311602 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311602 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311602 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca82624e-361e-5ab0-8f3d-deb7e2b73fe4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311602 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-1=0 \\\\ 2x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding both equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n3x-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+2y-1=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186311617 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311617 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311617 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311617 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{3}(t)+x(t)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311617 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9eb2e7ac-d5ca-5678-9359-e4e2a4d80ebd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311617 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+x-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=1-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+x-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+(1-y)-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y(y-1)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,\\text{ }0,\\text{ }1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $x:$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=1-0=1.$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get $x=1-1=0.$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get $x=1-(-1)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\text{and}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186429544 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429544 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: differential=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/differential-frequency-estimates.json, pair 6377->2086. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429544 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048451 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429545 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429545 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fr